<article class="env-lemma" id="0DYD">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0DYD">Lemma <span data-tag="0DYD">15.11.9</span>.</a> Let $I \subset J \subset A$ be ideals of a ring $A$. The following are equivalent </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$(A, I)$ and $(A/I, J/I)$ are henselian pairs, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$(A, J)$ is an henselian pair. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Assume (1). Let $B$ be an integral $A$-algebra. Consider the ring maps 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  B \to B/IB \to B/JB  \]
</div>
    <p>
       By Lemma <a href="/tag/09XI" data-tag="09XI">15.11.6</a> we find that both arrows induce bijections on idempotents. Hence so does the composition. Whence $(A, J)$ is a henselian pair by Lemma <a href="/tag/09XI" data-tag="09XI">15.11.6</a>. 
    <p>
      Conversely, assume (2) holds. Then $(A/I, J/I)$ is a henselian pair by Lemma <a href="/tag/09XK" data-tag="09XK">15.11.8</a>. Let $B$ be an integral $A$-algebra. Consider the ring maps 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  B \to B/IB \to B/JB  \]
</div>
    <p>
       By Lemma <a href="/tag/09XI" data-tag="09XI">15.11.6</a> we find that the composition and the second arrow induce bijections on idempotents. Hence so does the first arrow. It follows that $(A, I)$ is a henselian pair (by the lemma again). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>